// expect-exit: 0
//
// PROBE 06 — a generic `Vec<T>` with `move`, at a scalar `T` and an owning one.
//
// ASKS: does the container TM-005 and `BUILD.md` B-12 specify compile and work
// -- a generic struct over a `wild T->` block, `move T:x` into it, `#size_of<T>`
// arithmetic for the growth, and `ralloc` doubling -- at BOTH a scalar element
// type and one that owns heap memory?
//
// DEPENDS ON THE ANSWER: `Vec<T>` is the library's ONE container (B-13: no hash
// map, no second sequence), `Layout` stores `Vec<FmtPart>`, and cycle 0.0.4 is
// the subcycle that builds it. Its API table is `0.0.4.md` §2.
//
// THE SHAPE IS THE COMPILER'S OWN `List<T>` (src/frontend/list.npk, D-209),
// deliberately: B-12 says ours is that shape "because that shape is right and
// has been exercised across twenty-two families", and ours because a library
// must not import a compiler's internals. So this probe is not asking whether
// the shape works in general -- it is asking whether it works when WE write it,
// which is a different question only in that a typo makes it a different one.
//
// ==========================================================================
// WHAT THIS PROBE FOUND, AND IT WAS NOT WHAT IT WAS LOOKING FOR
// ==========================================================================
//
// FREEING THE BLOCK DOES NOT DROP THE ELEMENTS, AND `exit 0` DOES NOT NOTICE.
//
// `0.0.4.md` P-23 says "every `Vec` has a `vec_free` and every scope that made
// one calls it, or `exit 0` traps under D-151". That is true of the BLOCK and
// says nothing about the ELEMENTS: D-151 watches `wild` allocations, and a
// `string`'s body is managed, so a `Vec<string>` whose block is freed and whose
// elements are not is a leak that exits 0.
//
// Measured, 2 000 000 iterations of {init, push one 35-byte string, free}:
//
//   block freed, elements orphaned      125 184 KiB peak RSS   exit 0
//   elements moved out first, then free           0 KiB        exit 0
//
// and, because a peak-RSS number is arguable and an exit code is not, the same
// pair under `ulimit -v 65536` (a 64 MiB address space):
//
//   block freed, elements orphaned      exit 92   -- HeapOom, the runtime's arm
//   elements moved out first            exit 0
//
// ==========================================================================
// RE-MEASURED 2026-09-04 AT THE RE-PIN. The numbers above are BEFORE-numbers
// and they are UNCHANGED -- read that as the finding it is.
// ==========================================================================
//
// The compiler's D-246 (statement-end temporaries) and D-247 (`List<T>` as
// owning) both landed in 1.5.1b, and a leak measurement taken before them is
// exactly the kind of number a re-pin invalidates. So it was re-run rather
// than carried: three runs each at pin `94874ce`.
//
//   block freed, elements orphaned      125 184 KiB, all three runs   exit 0
//   elements moved out first                    0 KiB, all three      exit 0
//   under `ulimit -v 65536`:            exit 92 (HeapOom)  /  exit 0
//
// IDENTICAL, to the kilobyte. And it SHOULD be: D-246 fixes an unbound
// temporary, and this string is `move`d into the `Vec` -- consumed, never a
// temporary; D-247 makes the COMPILER's `List<T>` owning, which is a different
// type from this library's `Vec<T>`. So TM-106 and `SAFETY.md` S-18b stand
// exactly as written, and the obligation is still the library's.
//
// ONE NUMBER ABOVE IS AN INSTRUMENT ARTIFACT, and it is worth more than the
// leak. "0 KiB" is not a measurement: `/usr/bin/time -f %M` reports 0 for
// these static binaries -- `probe11d_floor_only`, a four-line program, also
// reports 0, while `/bin/true` reports 1256 KiB. So the remedy half's cost was
// never measured at all; it was read off a broken gauge that happened to say
// something believable. Re-done as an address-space bisection, which is an
// exit code rather than a gauge: the corrected half completes 2 000 000
// iterations in UNDER 768 KiB (clean at 768, `HeapOom` at 512), and the
// leaking half needs between 64 and 128 MiB. Those are the numbers to quote.
//
// AND THE PAIR IS NOW COMMITTED, which it was not before: this measurement
// lived in scratch on 2026-09-03 and only the numbers were committed, so at
// the re-pin there was nothing to re-run and the pair had to be rebuilt from
// the prose. They are `probe06b_element_leak.npk` and
// `probe06c_element_drop.npk`, they differ in one line, and the next re-pin
// costs one command.
//
// THE FIX IS ORDINARY CODE and it is in `free_names` below: move each element
// into a scoped local, which drops at the end of its iteration. It is not a
// language gap. What it is, is an obligation that three entries in `0.0.4.md`
// §2's API table silently carry -- `vec_free`, `vec_clear` and `vec_truncate`
// all discard elements, and all three of their stated postconditions talk only
// about `count`, `cap` and `items`. TM-106 and `SAFETY.md` S-18b are written
// against that.
//
// NOT ASKED HERE: an inherent `impl:<T>:Vec<T>` with a mutating receiver. §4
// listed it as a fallback to measure beside the free-function form, and
// `0.0.4.md` §2 has since settled the API as free functions -- `vec_init<T>`,
// `vec_push<T>` and the rest -- following the compiler's `list.npk`. A probe
// for a form the design does not use would pin a fact nothing depends on. The
// call syntax the free-function form needs IS pinned here, because it is not
// guessable: an explicit generic argument is a TURBOFISH, `vec_init::<int64>(2)`,
// and `vec_init<int64>(2)` is `NITPICK-PARSE-002`.
mod:probe06_generic_vec;

// `BUILD.md` B-12's shape, verbatim.
struct:Vec<T> = {
    wild T->:items;
    int64:count;
    int64:cap;
};

// A capacity of zero would make the doubling below a no-op forever, so the
// floor is one -- the compiler's `list_init` makes the same choice for the
// same reason.
func:vec_init<T> = Vec<T>(int64:cap) never fails {
    int64:n = cap;
    if (n < 1i64) { n = 1i64; }
    wild int8->:mem = alloc(n * #size_of<T>());
    pass Vec{ items: mem =>! wild T->, count: 0i64, cap: n };
};

// Room for `need` MORE elements. Doubling in a LOOP, not once: one doubling of
// a capacity-2 vector does not hold a bulk append of ten.
func:vec_reserve<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, int64:need) never fails {
    int64:want = v.count + need;
    if (want <= v.cap) { pass NIL; }
    int64:nc = v.cap;
    while (nc < want) { nc = nc * 2i64; }
    wild int8->:bigger = ralloc(v.items =>! wild int8->, nc * #size_of<T>());
    v.items = bigger =>! wild T->;
    v.cap = nc;
    pass NIL;
};

// `move T:x` -- the parameter is CONSUMING (D-208), which is what an owning
// element needs and what a scalar one is indifferent to. This is the line the
// probe exists for: the same generic function, at `int64` and at `string`.
func:vec_push<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, move T:x) never fails {
    drop vec_reserve(v, 1i64);
    v.items[v.count] = move(x);
    v.count = v.count + 1i64;
    pass NIL;
};

// Frees the BLOCK. Read the header: for an owning `T` the caller must have
// emptied it first, and nothing here can do that generically -- moving an
// element out needs a destination of type `T`, and a generic function has no
// scope in which a `T` may simply die.
func:vec_free<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v) never fails {
    dalloc(v.items =>! wild int8->);
    v.count = 0i64;
    v.cap = 0i64;
    pass NIL;
};

// THE OBLIGATION, DISCHARGED, at the one element type this probe owns. Each
// element is moved into a local whose scope ends at the bottom of the loop
// body, and the string dies there. Measured above, and RE-MEASURED at the
// re-pin: this is the difference between 125 MiB and under 768 KiB -- and
// between `HeapOom` and exit 0 under a 64 MiB cap, which is the half of the
// statement that does not depend on a gauge.
func:free_names = NIL(Vec<string>->:v) never fails {
    int64:i = 0i64;
    while (i < v.count) {
        string:owned = move(v.items[i]);
        discard(string_byte_length(owned));
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    v.count = 0i64;
    drop vec_free(v);
    pass NIL;
};

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) {
    // ---- a scalar T ---------------------------------------------------

    // Two, so that the third push must grow the block and `ralloc` is on the
    // measured path rather than a branch nothing took.
    Vec<int64>:a = raw vec_init::<int64>(2i64);
    if (a.cap != 2i64)                                  { exit 10i32; }
    if (a.count != 0i64)                                { exit 11i32; }

    drop vec_push(@a, 10i64);
    drop vec_push(@a, 20i64);
    if (a.cap != 2i64)                                  { exit 12i32; }
    drop vec_push(@a, 30i64);
    if (a.count != 3i64)                                { exit 13i32; }
    if (a.cap < 3i64)                                   { exit 14i32; }

    // The elements survived the reallocation, in order.
    if (a.items[0i64] != 10i64)                         { exit 15i32; }
    if (a.items[1i64] != 20i64)                         { exit 16i32; }
    if (a.items[2i64] != 30i64)                         { exit 17i32; }

    // The growth arithmetic is `#size_of<T>()`'s, and for `int64` that is 8.
    if (#size_of<int64>() != 8i64)                      { exit 18i32; }

    drop vec_free(@a);
    if (a.cap != 0i64)                                  { exit 19i32; }

    // ---- an OWNING T --------------------------------------------------

    // `string_concat` allocates, so each element owns a heap body. A
    // binding-to-binding copy of one is refused (TYPE-046); `move T:x` is how
    // it gets in, and this is the instantiation that proves the parameter
    // qualifier does its job.
    Vec<string>:b = raw vec_init::<string>(1i64);
    drop vec_push(@b, string_concat("Europe/", "Kyiv"));
    drop vec_push(@b, string_concat("America/", "Lima"));
    drop vec_push(@b, string_concat("Australia/", "Perth"));
    if (b.count != 3i64)                                { exit 20i32; }
    if (b.cap < 3i64)                                   { exit 21i32; }

    // Read back through the block: the bodies survived two reallocations, and
    // `ralloc` moved the 16-byte string headers without disturbing what they
    // point at.
    if (string_byte_length(b.items[0i64]) != 11i64)     { exit 22i32; }
    if (string_byte_length(b.items[1i64]) != 12i64)     { exit 23i32; }
    if (string_byte_length(b.items[2i64]) != 15i64)     { exit 24i32; }

    // A `string` is a 24-byte header -- MEASURED, not assumed; the first
    // draft of this line said 16, reasoning from a slice's `{ ptr, len }`,
    // and a `string` carries a third word. It is here because the block
    // arithmetic is `n * #size_of<T>()` and a wrong element size is an
    // overlapping write that no bound check catches.
    if (#size_of<string>() != 24i64)                    { exit 25i32; }

    // Empty it properly, then free the block. Swapping these two lines is the
    // leak the header measures.
    drop free_names(@b);
    if (b.count != 0i64)                                { exit 26i32; }
    if (b.cap != 0i64)                                  { exit 27i32; }

    // D-151: exit 0 additionally asserts that no `wild` allocation is live.
    // It does NOT assert that no managed body leaked -- see the header.
    exit 0i32;
};

func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (HeapBadRequest) { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)        { exit 92i32; },
        (IntOverflow)    { exit 93i32; },
        (OutOfBounds)    { exit 94i32; },
        (Unreachable)    { exit 95i32; },
        (WildLeak)       { exit 96i32; },
        (*)              { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
